|> Exercise
    handle=_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_

    Show that for all $A, B \in \rr$ there exists
    $W$, $\psi \in \rr$ such that

    $$
    A\cos x + B\sin x = W\cdot\cos(x - \psi)
    $$

    for all $x \in \rr$.

    |> Solution

        Starting from the desired endpoint, note that

        $$
        \cos(x - \psi) = \cos x\cos\psi + \sin x \sin \psi
        $$

        by the “angle-difference formula” (cf. Exercise >>_unit_dot_product_geometric_interpretation_) so

        $$
        W\cdot\cos(x - \psi)
        $$

        equals

        $$
        (W\cos\psi)\cdot\cos x + (W\sin\psi)\cdot\sin x
        $$

        where we suggestively group the terms to underscore
        that all we need to do in order to “win” is to satisfy 
        the equations 

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        A &=         W\cos\psi,\\
        B &= \up{1.5}W\rt{0.1}\sin\psi\,
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        and this can be done simply by setting $(W, \psi)$ to
        be the polar coordinates of the cartesian point $(A, B) \in \rr^2$!
        I.e., put

        $$
        W = \sqrt{A^2 + B^2}
        $$

        and...

        $$
        \psi = \begin{cases}
        \arctan(B/A)&\te{if }\, A \ne 0, \,\te{else} \\
        \up{1.2}\te{arccot}(A/B) &\te{if }\, B \ne 0, \,\te{else} \\
        \up{1.2}\rm{anything} &\te{if }\, A = 0, B = 0
        \end{cases}
        $$

        ...where we copy-paste a formula from the chapter. 
        (Or just 
        |> span
            class=code-cartouche
            psi = atan2(B, A)
        \ if we were writing python.\*) (\*Indeed,
        “$\psi$” is “psi”.)
